{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c401","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c9a4-cdaf-7691-9123-20534004d71c"},"sequence":91,"recorded_at":1780531400000177,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"7d4d0da9-54d0-407b-9491-ce60f372e27f","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"7d4d0da9-54d0-407b-9491-ce60f372e27f","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"7d4d0da9-54d0-407b-9491-ce60f372e27f"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c9a5-0ae2-7970-b4f8-e6f1ca51c52d"},"task_id":"01a0c9a5-0ae2-7970-b4f8-e6f1ca51c52d","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c9a5-0ae2-7970-b4f8-e6f1ca51c52d","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev9.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev8.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz. Revision 8 was reviewed by RUN-260922-d423e7 (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: **one P1**, four P2. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev8.md first.\n3|**Revision 8 did the hard part.** The reviewer confirms it correctly revalidates before escalation — the stale-facts kill is gone — and correctly bounds the attach admission and barrier waits. The narrowed scope held: the overlap liveness subsystem stays assigned to TASK-260922-vcx6yo and nothing in this review expands the leaf back into it. Keep all of it. What remains is one fail-open read boundary, the same waiting contract at one more site, a replay case, and two evidence defects.\n4|\n5|1. **P1 — your new peer census reads a corrupt receipt as absence.** `Execute(attach) → executeAttach → checkAttachOverlap → AttachStore.Peers` (`internal/termbind/overlap.go:47`) skips **every** directory before attempting a read, including a directory sitting at the exact `<client UUID>.json` receipt path. That is unreadable receipt evidence, not an unrelated staging file and not proof the client is gone. `checkAttachOverlap` then reads `len(peers)==0` at `ops.go:298` and admits a second client with `multi_attach` absent. `TestReviewAttachPeerDirectoryFailsClosed` drives a valid writable attach, replaces that client's receipt file with a directory of the same name keeping the original bytes inside, then drives a different writable client: success, second receipt persisted, `InputAuthorized:true`. Fails twice under `-race`, no race diagnostic, no real tmux. Your own B44 defers **liveness** and explicitly promises that unreadable peers fail closed — it does not authorise treating a corrupt receipt-shaped entry as an empty census. Distinguish legitimate staging and non-receipt entries from invalid objects **inside the receipt namespace**, refuse unknown or corrupt receipt entries before committing a new attach, and ship the production-entry negative plus a narrowing witness. The client-liveness handoff stays intact.\n6|2. **P2-A — the stop probe still waits past the operation deadline.** `backend.go:453` passes the original caller context to the blocking runner; `executeEngineOp` computes a deadline into a field but never gives this read-only probe a deadline-bounded context, and `OSRunner` uses `exec.CommandContext`, so it can only cancel on the context it is handed. Clock checks after `Run` cannot cancel a `Run` that has not returned. With a real clock, a 200 ms operation deadline and hour-long authorization, `has-session` stays blocked until an external release at ~600 ms and only then returns `stop_timeout` (measured 619 ms and 621 ms). This is revision 7's deadline-cancels-waiting requirement at the polling entry rather than at attach. B29 discloses unmeasured poll instants; it does not document an intentional unbounded subprocess. Apply the operation and graceful-wait contract to the **actual polling command context**, audit every other runner wait through the same shared contract, and keep the no-kill-after-stale-facts repair. One more thing, stated separately because it is a correctness rule and not a timing one: **a read-only poll timing out must never manufacture process closure** — absence has to be positively observed.\n7|3. **P2-B — the same-client replay exemption only works when the client is alone.** `checkAttachOverlap` excludes the requesting client from the peer list but never checks whether that client already holds a valid receipt, so a replay with another peer recorded still demands overlap capabilities before the durable store can replay. Your `TestAttachSameClientRetryIgnoresOverlap` uses a lone client, so its name and the B44, row-79 and results claims outrun what it measures: attach A and B with the full capability set, then replay A's identical body with only `local_attach` — the exact reduced-capability shape your committed retry test uses — and it returns `terminal_backend_capability_unproven` instead of replaying. The exemption holds for one of the two populations, not both. Distinguish a **validated identical replay** from a new client at the overlap admission boundary, preserving authorization, generation and integrity checks; test replay with another peer present in both read-only and input-authorized shapes; and do not infer replay from the caller's client ID alone.\n8|4. **P2-C — the attached producer archive is revision 3.** `TASK-260830-1c28dz_producer-evidence.tar.gz` on the board is a real gzip archive whose embedded results begin \"Producer results, rev3\", describe 41 changed paths and carry 235 verdict rows across four harness chunks, while your current standalone results describe revision 8, 48 paths, 293 tmuxserver rows and 41 termbind rows. There is no revision-8 archive in the scoped inventory. Different candidates: the old archive cannot substantiate the current per-plant, raw-log and composition claims. (The CR8 validation attachment is fine and reports 30/30 green — this finding is only about the detailed producer evidence.) Attach the actual current-candidate archive by absolute path, **read the resource back, verify its digest and contents**, and make the standalone results cite those exact bytes. Keep the earlier archive under its real revision rather than presenting it as current.\n9|5. **P2-D — your kill attribution claims paths that survive when isolated.** The new TRACEABILITY symmetry line claims `stop 4 / quiesce 4` for effectRecheck and the waitbound row claims four killer paths. A shared mutant's process failing does not establish that all those paths killed it. Both harness passes show generation failing through stop and quiesce, but `N-stop-escalation-expiry` and `N-stop-escalation-deadline` fail only through stop: quiesce advances 12:00 → 14:00 against a 13:00 expiry, so the minus-one-hour mutation still rejects at exact expiry, and its deadline case is strictly past the deadline so it cannot kill an equal-instant narrowing. The boundary-commit test advances seven hours against six hours remaining, so adding an hour in `N-attach-wait-deadline` leaves the wait immediately expired and that test does not kill the declared widening. Rerun with only the claimed killer selected and **3 of 3 isolated attributions SURVIVE twice**. Measured reality is **stop 4 / quiesce 2** and **3 of 4** wait-bound kill paths. Either add the rows that make the claimed attributions true, or restate the numbers to what isolation measures — and from now on attribute a kill by selecting that killer alone, never by reading a shared plant's process exit.\n10|\n11|PROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on top of the checkpoint; base `d4bd91d0`, and if trunk moves refresh through the managed path and repeat the path-by-path audit. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property. Run your own package tests, the full importer closure, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table, the runner-wait audit from item 2, and the isolated kill attributions from item 5; update the conformance matrix, attach the **current** evidence archive and the standalone results, verify every attachment by reading it back off the board and comparing digests, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 9 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\n12|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n13|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n14|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n15|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n16|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n17|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n18|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n19|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","final_result":true}}}
